# two_stretches_in_one_scope_are_two_grounds
#
# The same world `one_scope_two_stretches_is_two_wants` derives from, seen from the other end:
# tank1 in range now, its level dropping at 12:30 and its temperature rising at 13:00, both
# predicates in ONE scope. That case asserts the two troubles are two WANTS; this one asserts
# they are three GROUNDS, and that the third holds both changes.
#
# THE DIFFS ACCUMULATE, which is the claim worth a case. Each ground is forked from the one
# BEFORE it, not from the present, so the 13:00 ground carries the dip that began at 12:30 as
# well as the heat that begins now. Forked from the present each time, the last ground would
# hold a risen temperature beside a level that had never dropped — a world that is neither
# what the agent believes nor what it foresees.
#
# THREE PERIODS, NOT TWO, because each prediction changes something: neither hashes the same
# as the ground before it. `a_forecast_that_says_nothing_new_is_no_period` is the other half —
# a forecast restating the present is no period at all.
#
# NOTHING IS IN ONE SCOPE HERE AS FAR AS THE FILL IS CONCERNED, and that is the point of
# saying it: a scope names WHICH imaginarium this is and does not cut what crosses into it.
# Narrowing the fill per scope was built, measured at 10.4 ms against 0.6 on a pass costing
# over a second, and refused — the lean set fails by returning an EMPTY RESULT rather than an
# error, and fewer graphs is the same move under another name.
#
# The clock stands at 2026-01-01T12:00:00Z.

@prefix : <http://example.org/test#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

GRAPH :ontology {
  orexis:StateGraph rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph .
  orexis:PredictionGraph rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph .
}

GRAPH :world {
  :keeper a orexis:Agent .
  :tank1 a :Tank .
}

GRAPH :sensed {
  :tank1 :level 20 .
  :tank1 :temperature 20 .
}

#  THE LEVEL DROPS AT HALF PAST — and takes the standing reading with it.
GRAPH :dip {
  :tank1 :level 5 .
}

#  THE TEMPERATURE RISES AN HOUR IN. It says nothing about the level, so the level the dip
#  left must survive into this ground: that is what forking from the one before does.
GRAPH :heat {
  :tank1 :temperature 40 .
}

GRAPH :catalogue {
  :catalogue a orexis:CatalogueGraph .
  :catalogue a orexis:Graph .
  :ontology a orexis:Graph .
  :ontology a orexis:OntologyGraph .
  :ontology a orexis:PublicGraph .
  :world a orexis:Graph .
  :world a orexis:PublicGraph .
  :sensed a orexis:Graph .
  :sensed a orexis:StateGraph .
  :sensed orexis:beliefsOf :keeper .
  :dip a orexis:Graph .
  :dip a orexis:PredictionGraph .
  :dip orexis:beliefsOf :keeper .
  :dip dcterms:temporal [ a dcterms:PeriodOfTime ; orexis:start "2026-01-01T12:30:00+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ] .
  :dip orexis:retracts """PREFIX : <http://example.org/test#>
      CONSTRUCT { :tank1 :level ?l }
      WHERE { GRAPH $state { :tank1 :level ?l } }""" .
  :heat a orexis:Graph .
  :heat a orexis:PredictionGraph .
  :heat orexis:beliefsOf :keeper .
  :heat dcterms:temporal [ a dcterms:PeriodOfTime ; orexis:start "2026-01-01T13:00:00+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ] .
  :heat orexis:retracts """PREFIX : <http://example.org/test#>
      CONSTRUCT { :tank1 :temperature ?t }
      WHERE { GRAPH $state { :tank1 :temperature ?t } }""" .
}
